<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dwight D. Eisenhower</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a>–<a href="/years/1969">1969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Eisenhower</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dwight D. Eisenhower was a US general and President of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from 1953 to 1961. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a> of 1956 he pressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to withdraw from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> by threatening to withhold financial support for the pound, a humiliation that exposed the limits of British power and accelerated the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower.jpg" alt="Dwight D. Eisenhower" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: White House · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dwight_D._Eisenhower,_official_photo_portrait,_May_29,_1959_(cropped)(3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eisenhower was born David Dwight Eisenhower in Denison, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, on 14 October 1890, the third of seven sons of Ida Stover and David Jacob Eisenhower. His mother soon reversed his forenames to avoid two Davids in the family. All the boys were nicknamed Ike, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> only Dwight still was. He graduated from West Point in 1915. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> his request for an overseas assignment was denied, and he commanded a unit that trained tank crews at Camp Colt, his first command; he never saw combat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He was promoted to brigadier general on 3 October 1941. In November 1942 he was appointed commander of the Allied forces in the North African theatre, in the campaign designated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-torch">Operation Torch</a>. In December 1943 President Roosevelt decided that he, not Marshall, would be Supreme Allied Commander in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and he planned and carried out the Normandy assault of June 1944, Operation Overlord. After the German surrender he was military governor of the American-occupied zone of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and he ordered camera crews to document the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-concentration-camps">Nazi concentration camps</a> for the Nuremberg Trials. He then served as Army Chief of Staff, president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbia-university">Columbia University</a> from 1948, and first supreme commander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>.</p><p>He entered the 1952 presidential race as a Republican, in part to block Senator Robert A. Taft, who opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>, and won that election and the 1956 election, 457 to 73 in the electoral vote, against Adlai Stevenson II. As president he gave the Atoms for Peace speech to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> on 8 December 1953, and in 1956 he signed the bill authorising the Interstate Highway System. After the Soviet launch of Sputnik he led the American response, which included NASA. Alaska and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> were admitted as states in 1959. He died on 28 March 1969, aged 78.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His New Look policy relied on nuclear deterrence, building a stockpile of nuclear weapons and delivery systems to deter threats and save money by cutting expensive Army combat units. In his first State of the Union address in February 1953 he said he would use the authority of the presidency to end segregation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">District of Columbia</a> and the Armed Forces, and when the services resisted he used government control of military spending to force the change. In his farewell address he warned of the dangers of massive military spending, which he called the military–industrial complex.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dwight D. Eisenhower” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>President <b>Eisenhower</b>'s administration refused support for sterling and blocked British access to IMF funds unless Britain withdrew from Egypt, and Prime Minister Anthony Eden's climbdown showed that Britain could no longer pursue an independent great-power policy against American wishes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1957 President <b>Dwight D. Eisenhower</b> sent paratroopers to escort nine Black students into Central High School in Little Rock, Arkansas, after Governor Orval Faubus had used the National Guard to bar them.</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Some 56,000 people had died there; <b>Eisenhower</b> later had local Germans brought to see it.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-11">11 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>President <b>Eisenhower</b> proclaimed Hawaii's admission as the 50th state.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-21">21 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The next day it broadcast a recorded Christmas message from President <b>Eisenhower</b>, the first voice transmitted from space.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-18">18 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>First Lady Mamie <b>Eisenhower</b> christened Nautilus at Groton, Connecticut.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-21">21 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Eisenhower Birthplace State Historic Site, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Walter Reed Army Medical Center, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.75,-96.53&amp;year=1941" aria-label="Eisenhower Birthplace State Historic Site on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2087 940 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Dwight D. Eisenhower"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2537 1210h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2537 1210h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.75,-96.53&amp;year=1941">What was here in 1941? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;IN THE COUNCILS of government, we must guard against the acquisition of unwarranted influence, whether sought or unsought, by the military-industrial complex.&#8221;</p><footer>Farewell Address (1961) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Eisenhower%27s_farewell_address_(reading_copy)" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Only an alert and knowledgeable citizenry can compel the proper meshing of the huge industrial and military machinery of defense with our peaceful methods and goals, so that security and liberty may prosper together.&#8221;</p><footer>Farewell Address (1961) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Eisenhower%27s_farewell_address_(reading_copy)" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Every gun that is made, every warship launched, every rocket fired signifies, in the final sense, a theft from those who hunger and are not fed, those who are cold and are not clothed.&#8221;</p><footer>The Chance for Peace, address to the American Society of Newspaper Editors (1953) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Chance_for_Peace" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
